A||N||14||007DOUG||88 coupe runs like a clogged cat, only its not clogged!!!||||1||
Z||000000||007DOUG||02-20-2001||07:40 PM||||88 4 cyl 5 speed coupe with a bad knock:<P>I replaced the bad 4 banger with a good 10K (on previous rebuild) 88 engine. I used all the sensors from the known good 4 banger.<BR> <BR>She tried to start, but wouldn't run.<P>Installed new crank sensor and coilpacs.<P>She now starts (sometimes). Runs with a  slight miss at idle, is hard to get re-started, and has ZERO power. All the symptoms of all clogged cat. I replaced the cat, and the problem is still here.<P>Any suggestions? I'm ready to toss this 88 out the window.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Richard Parnell||02-20-2001||07:54 PM||||Check the O2 sensor and wire.  Mine when I had a 4 banger had no power and ran real hot with the O2 sensor disconctd.||||reg||1
Z||000002||danny boy||02-20-2001||08:12 PM||||  Check the rest of the exaust. When I first got my '87 4cly it ran but had no power,missed at idle and the cat would glow red after a few minutes. turns out some of the cat insides got broke out and had the muffler clogged. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||02-20-2001||11:39 PM||||Believe it or not, I had the same kind of problems. Computer kicked back a lean 02 code. Changed the o2 sesor and still had problem. Checked fuel pressure, just had flow. Pulled the gas tank to find the 3" long rubber hose from the pump to the steel line had split longitudinally, allowing partial bypass.||||reg||1
Z||000004||007DOUG||02-21-2001||12:12 AM||||Has brand new cat, and zero check engine codes. O2 is good.<P>She's getting plenty of gas too. When she idles, the gas just pours through. Previous owner installed a new fuel pump. At idle, I can run the RPMs upto 4K with no problem, but it sounds sluggish even at high rpm.<P>I'm pretty much down to two parts......the ignition module or computer. But, its always been my understanding that when the mentioned parts go bad, the engine won't run at all. Right?<P>Thanx, Doug  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||billyboy||02-21-2001||01:34 AM||||Hehe... When I first read the title, I thought he meant constapated feline (clogged cat)... Doh...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||2
Z||000006||TheFiero64||02-21-2001||11:32 AM||||My 88 coupe did that to me left me stranded 40 miles from home. I replaced the crank sensor and coil packs. I also was going to have the module replaced but the parts place tested it and said it was fine. I was about to give up then was told to put low resistance plug wires. I argued because the old ones were only 6 months old. I replaced them and it ran great until I replaced it with the V6.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||3
Z||000007||DavidHenshaw||02-21-2001||08:41 PM||||Don't give up, its probably something simple.<P>Check to be sure the correct spark plug wire is going to the correct cylinder. I don't knowhat the firing order on a 4 cyl is but if your plug wires are off you could be trying to run on only two cylinders. <P>I assume all your plug wires are solidly seated on the plugs. Don't laugh, I got a V8 back from a garage once and they had forgot to attach two of the new plug wires. I found one of them after a couple days and thought man that runs better. Then I found the other one after another week and it really ran good.<P>Dave<P>86 GT<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||DavidHenshaw||02-21-2001||08:49 PM||||One other thing Doug. You might check to be sure the timing mark on the crank is properly alligned with the mark on the camshaft. If its out by much intake or exhaust valves could be opening or closing too soon or too late. <P>If the previous owner can assure you it ran ok with power during the first 10K miles then you can eliminate the above possibility.<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||007DOUG||02-24-2001||10:07 PM||||While I'm waiting for my computer from FieroDave, let me rule out another possible problem........the sparkplug arrangement.<P>Lets say the cylinder nearest the waterpump is #1, and work our way across to #4.<P>Here's the order I have the plug wires on the coilpacks.....2(top),4,3,1(bottom)<P>Please, please, please tell be wrong. Please be that simple.<P>If another computer doesn't solve.....I swear I'll toss this car out the window. It is Unfixable!!!!!<P>Thanx, doug||||reg||5
Z||000010||MrPBody||02-24-2001||10:16 PM||||In old posts, I have read that some spark plugs (Bosch platinum?) work poorly with 87-88 DIS ignition.  I don't know from personal experience.  (I have an '85.)<P>Since it's a known problem and an easy fix, I thought I would mention it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||02-25-2001||12:27 AM||||DIS 4 cyl fire order 1432 1,4 on bottom coil 2,3 on top.<A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||007DOUG||02-25-2001||09:57 AM||||Man, I don't know how I could have reversed those wires????? I feel like such an amateur. Does start easier now.<P>BUT.......she's still got zero power, and runs with a miss. This new computer has to fix it. If not, shes a lemon.<P>Got brand new AC plugs.||||reg||11
Z||000013||theogre||02-25-2001||12:22 PM||||I hate to tell you this but the computer is likely not your problem. The DIS version of this motor is extremely reliable but also very picky about the condition of it's controls. If one sensor is screwed up, even a little, you will have problems. You need to reset the computer now that the wires are where they belong.<P>Check the MAT sensor to the right of the TBI. If it's backfired thru the intake that sensor is likely fried.<P>make sure none of the vac hoses are out of place. Make sure the MAP is connected to the correct TBI port and the line is good. If the 2 vac lines for the fuel vapor can are wrong you'll have a vacuum problem.<P>make sure there is no leak on the PCV line. all 4 elbow jounts should be clamped on that line. be gentle with the clamps or you'll ruin the elbows.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Scott_M||02-25-2001||12:24 PM||||I had the exact same problem two months after buying my 87 SE 4 cyl. It turns out it was the ignition module. After replacing everything else under the sun, I only had enough money left over to by one, but it worked. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Signature.jpg">   <BR>87 SE 4cyl,5 spd. manual||||reg||1
